[2026-11-17] r:5936936421 review COMMENTED by enochaz (contributor) (edited 2026-11-17):
Concept ACK

As I understand it, `m_blocks_unlinked` is used both to reconsider blocks for `setBlockIndexCandidates` when a missing ancestor arrives and to set `m_chain_tx_count` for their descendants. If B's transactions were received but an ancestor’s were not, pruning B clears `BLOCK_HAVE_DATA` without clearing `nTx`.

Removing B from the map at that point means that when the ancestor arrives, we no longer reach B or any blocks waiting behind it. This seems unlikely in normal sync, but the stale fork case shows it can happen.

So I think it makes sense to keep B in the map while its `m_chain_tx_count` is still unknown, and to reconstruct that state on restart. B still shouldn’t become a chain candidate while its block data is missing.

The `nSequenceId` change also seems justified since it affects the candidate set’s ordering, though that feels like a separate implementation detail.

